<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Once upon a Time... – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Once upon a Time...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present their views on simple issues. Interactive: Respond to classmates' presentations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present views on topics within the legend (e.g., "The hero was brave because he helped others."). Can respond to classmates' ideas about Panamanian legends and ask follow-up questions (e.g., "I agree with you. La Tulivieja was terrifying. What would you have done if you had heard her crying by the river at night?"). Can describe short stories or legends to peers (e.g., "There once was a clever rabbit.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of popular Panamanian legend characters (e.g., La Tulivieja, El Chivato, the Golden Frog) on the board or screen. Students are asked to briefly share what they remember or know about these images. The teacher prompts with questions like, "Which legend do you see here? What was the creature doing?" to activate prior knowledge of vocabulary (legend, monster, creature, spirit) and the Past Simple. (2-3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models short sentences about a legend using target grammar and vocabulary. For example, pointing to La Tulivieja: "Once upon a time, La Tulivieja was crying near the river at night." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "-ing" in "crying" and the time marker "at night." The teacher then models a sentence with Past Simple: "Then, a person heard her and ran away bravely." The teacher models the pronunciation of the -ed ending in "heard" and "ran" (irregular past simple) and the adverb "bravely." (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and grammar usage. (1 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'The hero was fighting the monster,' was the action finished or still happening?" (Expected answer: "Still happening.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Last week, the monster appeared suddenly,' does 'last week' tell us *when*?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it tells us the time.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 5-6 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio narration (approx. 1-1.5 min) of an excerpt from a lesser-known Panamanian legend (e.g., "El Hombre Caimán" or "La India Dormida"), focusing on actions in the past. The narration uses past continuous and past simple, adverbs of time, sequencing words, and target vocabulary (e.g., `once upon a time`, `hero`, `river`, `mountain`, `explore`, `encounter`, `suddenly`, `appeared`, `was venturing`, `was fighting`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Clarify details in group discussions and present short stories or legends to peers (e.g., "The man was fighting the monster when the village was attacked.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice. (4 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> "What was the main character doing at the beginning of the story?" (e.g., "The man was exploring the mountain.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen):</b> Teacher provides 2-3 simple T/F statements based on the audio. E.g., "True or False: The hero was sleeping when the monster appeared." or "True or False: The village was quiet during the attack." Students say "True" or "False" and briefly justify their answer orally.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 6 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice speaking using Past continuous, Past simple, Adverbs of time (`yesterday`, `last month`, `last week`), sequencing words (`once upon a time`, `after`, `before`, `during`, `suddenly`, `one day`, `long ago`, `in the end`), and target vocabulary (nouns: `legend`, `monster`, `hero`, `village`, `town`, `river`, `mountain`; verbs: `fight`, `save`, `rescue`, `defend`, `explore`, `encounter`, `appear`, `disappear`, `transform`; adjectives: `brave`, `strong`, `mysterious`, `evil`; adverbs: `bravely`, `mysteriously`, `silently`, `at night`). Practice pronunciation of -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/) and -ing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> (15 min)</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Oral Completion (5 min):</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences on the board related to the listened legend or other known legends. Students work individually or in pairs to orally complete them, focusing on the correct tense and vocabulary.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Long ago, the brave hero ____ (fight) the ____ (evil creature)." (Expected: "fought", "evil creature")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The villagers ____ (celebrate) when the monster ____ (disappear)." (Expected: "celebrated", "disappeared" – practicing /ɪd/ pronunciation)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One day, while the girl ____ (explore) the ____ (ancient forest), she ____ (encounter) a spirit." (Expected: "was exploring", "ancient forest", "encountered" – practicing /d/ pronunciation)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Substitution Drill (5 min):</b> In pairs, students receive a sentence frame about a legend and take turns substituting underlined words with new target vocabulary. The teacher models first.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The `brave hero` was `fighting` the `monster` `bravely`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: "The `strong warrior` was `defending` the `village` `skillfully`."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: "The `mysterious creature` was `appearing` `silently` `at night`."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation of -ed/-ing endings and providing immediate feedback.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture Prompt Narration (5 min):</b> Students are shown 3-4 sequential pictures depicting a simple legend story (e.g., a hero seeing a creature, fighting it, and saving the village). In pairs, students take turns narrating what is happening in each picture, using past continuous and past simple, sequencing words (first, then, after, suddenly, in the end). They focus on describing actions and linking events. Example: "First, the hero was walking by the river. Suddenly, a monster appeared!"</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Storytelling and Opinion Sharing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> (15 min)</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each pair chooses a Panamanian legend they know (or a simplified version provided by the teacher).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Story Retelling (7 min):</b> Partner A narrates a segment of the chosen legend to Partner B, focusing on sequencing events and describing actions using Past Simple and Past Continuous (e.g., "Once upon a time, the spirit was guarding the hidden treasure on the mountain. One day, a man ventured into the area, exploring the ancient trees..."). Partner B listens actively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Opinion & Interaction (8 min):</b> After Partner A finishes, Partner B shares their view on an aspect of the legend (e.g., "I think the hero was brave because he tried to save the village.") and asks Partner A a follow-up question (e.g., "What would you have done if you encountered that creature?"). Then, they switch roles for another segment of the legend or a different legend. Students are encouraged to use phrases like "I agree with you," "I disagree," "In my opinion," and ask "Why?" to clarify details. The teacher circulates, encouraging interaction and natural communication.</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, demonstrating the ability to present views and respond to classmates' ideas about Panamanian legends.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Performance stage, focusing on individual student contributions in the pair interaction. A quick peer assessment at the end of the activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will have presented a segment of a legend and interacted by giving an opinion and asking a follow-up question. The teacher will listen for specific language use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially -ed and -ing endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates relevant nouns (e.g., `legend`, `hero`, `monster`), verbs (e.g., `fought`, `saved`, `was exploring`), adjectives (e.g., `brave`, `mysterious`), and adverbs (e.g., `bravely`, `at night`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Storytelling is easy to follow; opinions are expressed clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Past Simple/Continuous:</b> Accurately uses these tenses for narration.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Actively listens and responds to partner's ideas and questions appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> (4 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new word or phrase you used today when talking about a legend."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to ask your partner a follow-up question? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 4 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of telling a legend was most interesting to you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use Past Simple and Past Continuous to describe events in your own life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> (1 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The pair storytelling and opinion sharing went well, with most students attempting to use the target grammar and vocabulary. The interactive part of asking follow-up questions needed more scaffolding; some students struggled to formulate spontaneous questions beyond "Why?". For next time, I could provide sentence starters for follow-up questions or model more diverse questions during practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice of narrating legends and sequencing events will directly prepare students for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), where they will write their own short stories or legend summaries using the same grammatical structures and vocabulary. The focus on sequencing and coherent narrative will be crucial for effective writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>